$DIR/test.p:13:32: string(s) of a slice whose elements are not runes is not permitted in Plutonium (only the element type rune says that the integers are characters; use []rune, or write each rune(x) with a strings.Builder)
    string(i) of an integer does not give the digits of i. In Go it gives the
    character whose code is i, so string(65) is "A" and string(n) for n := 65 is
    "A" too, not "65". Go's own vet tool reports it because programmers expect
    the digits.
    Plutonium accepts a conversion to string from a value of type rune and from
    an untyped constant such as string('A'), and nothing else, so the choice is
    visible in the source. A defined type is not accepted even when it is
    defined as rune or int32 (type Fd int32), because string(Fd(3)) cannot be
    told from a conversion meant to write the number. To make the character,
    convert to a rune first: string(rune(i)). To make the digits, use
    strconv.Itoa(i), or strconv.FormatInt and strconv.FormatUint for other
    integer types.
    The same holds for a slice: string(s) accepts a slice whose element type is
    rune, whatever the name of the slice type, and not a slice of myRune or Fd.
    Write each element with rune(c) to a strings.Builder.
    A conversion that reads like formatting but means something else is a trap
    that Go itself warns about. Plutonium has no false friends: the program says
    which of the two it means.
    To write the characters whose codes are the elements, convert each one to a
    rune:
        import "pluto/strings"

        var b strings.Builder
        for _, c := range s {
            b.WriteRune(rune(c))
        }
        text := b.String()
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E2017" for a longer explanation)
